Newly confirmed credential and data-retention risks

Plaintext OAuth token persistence

OAuth access and refresh tokens are serialized to:

~/.crudo/credentials.json

Evidence:

rust/crates/runtime/src/oauth.rs:12-19
rust/crates/runtime/src/oauth.rs:283-291
rust/crates/runtime/src/oauth.rs:371-380

The credential-writing path does not reliably set restrictive file permissions before writing the temporary plaintext file.

Plaintext provider API keys

Provider API keys are written into settings:

rust/crates/runtime/src/config.rs:1111-1160

Permissions are applied only after writing, and the lower-level settings write path does not provide robust atomic permission handling.

Configuration read failures can be destructive

Unreadable or malformed settings are treated as an empty configuration:

rust/crates/runtime/src/config.rs:1185-1193

A later save can overwrite existing settings, masking the original read/parse failure.

For confidential industrial deployments, configuration parse/read failures should stop the operation rather than silently reset state.

Session redaction is incomplete

The session sanitizer handles a narrow set of patterns:

rust/crates/runtime/src/session.rs:1066-1194

It may miss:

- JSON-style apiKey,
- access_token,
- refresh_token,
- lowercase bearer,
- arbitrary token formats,
- credentials nested in tool outputs.

Additionally, Session::to_json can serialize unsanitized content blocks:

rust/crates/runtime/src/session.rs:360-410
rust/crates/runtime/src/session.rs:818-875

Telemetry does not guarantee redaction

Telemetry attributes and properties are persisted directly:

rust/crates/telemetry/src/lib.rs:159-203
rust/crates/telemetry/src/lib.rs:265-275

If callers place secrets in attributes, they can enter JSONL telemetry.

Provider errors can contain secrets

Provider error bodies are retained in API errors:

rust/crates/api/src/error.rs:55-66
rust/crates/api/src/error.rs:115-130
rust/crates/api/src/providers/anthropic.rs:388-391
rust/crates/api/src/providers/openai_compat.rs:230-252

These errors derive Debug/Display and may be placed into sessions or telemetry.

OAuth/auth structures also derive debug formatting while holding raw token strings:

rust/crates/api/src/providers/anthropic.rs:32-40,98-105

The masked authorization helper protects only one explicit display path; it does not make the underlying types safe to debug-format.



